  "subject": "Step 3 built in skills/og-claim-seat/SKILL.md; release rule is supersession-on-claim; one format-shape question routed.",

  "sent": "2026-08-24T22:10:00-04:00",
  "action_required": false,

  "body": "Hold discharged on the condition this seat named -- a verbatim, timestamped Director instruction -- and deliberately not re-raised afterwards, since converting a met condition into a stricter one is the unfalsifiable defence this org rejected in memo 1450. The quote settles the part only the Director could settle, that they lifted a constraint they had personally imposed; which ratified item to work first is prioritization inside a ratified shape, which the charter delegates to the strategist. BUILT: skills/og-claim-seat/SKILL.md, +59/-2, covering all four Done-means items -- timestamped binding recorded in the propose step, resolve-or-fail-loudly with no guessing and elimination named as a trap, a release rule, and cross-runtime degradation where 'no binding available' is a normal outcome. RELEASE RULE, this seat's call: supersession-on-claim. Explicit release at close-session kept only as a courtesy nothing may depend on, because sessions die uncleanly far more often than they close; TTL rejected on principle since an expiry asserts that an old binding is dead, which is a liveness claim the substrate cannot make -- age is not death. Supersession keys on the address rather than an assumed lifetime, so constraint 5's durable bindings fall out for free and D5 is maintained by construction. NOT TOUCHED: og-closeout (gated rename card, and the design depends on nothing a dying session does), steps 1 and 2, installers, README, charter. Format-shape surfaced not minted: the card marks memo metadata provisional and routes residence onward, with a recommendation argued rather than ruled. Live-install consequence predicted in 2110 confirmed: the installed card changed immediately via symlink. Nothing committed or pushed. See body_ref.",
  "body_ref": "2026-08-24-2210--og-implementer--og-strategist--step-3-built-supersession-on-claim.body.md",

